+# Model card — fraud-v3-candidate
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Model** | `fraud-v3-candidate` (transaction fraud scoring) |
+| **Champion** | `fraud-v2`, in production since 2026-02-12 |
+| **Status** | **Blocked.** Shadow promotion stopped on 2026-09-16; see *Validation history*. |
+| **Owner** | ml-fraud |
+| **Ticket** | TESS-2310 |
+| **Card written** | 2026-09-20, for MRM review under SR 11-7 |
+| **Reviewed by** | *(pending MRM)* |
+
+This card supersedes the numbers in `ml/registry/models.json` and
+`ml/notebooks/01_fraud_exploration.ipynb`. **The recorded offline result of
+AUC 0.906 / PR-AUC 0.358 is withdrawn: it was target leakage, not
+performance.** The honest figures are below, and they are level with the
+champion.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Purpose and scope
+
+Scores a card authorization for fraud risk, synchronously, on the
+authorization path. The score is consumed by `services/risk_gateway`, which
+fails closed: if scoring is unavailable or times out, the transaction is
+declined.
+
+**In scope.** Ranking authorizations for (a) automatic decline above the
+decline band and (b) placement in the manual review queue, which has a budget
+of roughly the top 1% of traffic.
+
+**Out of scope, and not validated for:**
+
+- credit decisions of any kind — limits, approvals, pricing. This model is
+  trained on fraud outcomes, not repayment, and carries no adverse-action
+  reason-code mapping (§8);
+- merchant-level risk scoring or merchant offboarding;
+- chargeback representment, dispute liability, or any use where the target is
+  the dispute rather than the fraud;
+- any population outside card-present and card-not-present authorizations on
+  Tessera-issued consumer cards.
+
+The most likely scope creep is the third one: the model is trained against
+`is_fraud`, and `chargeback_filed_at` is a *different and later* event. Do not
+repoint it at disputes without revalidating from §2 down.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Training data
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Source | `ml/data/generate.py` — **synthetic**, seed 20260917 |
+| Rows | 60,000 transactions |
+| Period | 2026-03-01 → 2026-08-27 (180 days) |
+| Entities | 6,000 cards, 400 merchants, 4,500 devices |
+| Base rate | 3.51% fraud (2,108 rows) |
+| Dispute coverage | 84.3% of fraud is eventually disputed (1,777 rows) |
+| Dispute lag | 20–89 days after the transaction, median 54 |
+
+There is no real cardholder data in this repository and there never will be.
+Every number in this card is computed on synthetic transactions; they
+establish that the pipeline is correct, not that the model performs in
+production. Production numbers come from shadow, which is where this candidate
+failed.
+
+**Split — temporal, not random.** Cut at the 70th percentile of `timestamp`,
+**2026-07-05 01:06:01 UTC**:
+
+| | rows | window | fraud rate |
+|---|---:|---|---:|
+| train | 42,000 | 2026-03-01 → 2026-07-05 | 3.51% |
+| scored | 18,000 | 2026-07-05 → 2026-08-27 | 3.53% |
+
+The scored window is deliberately the same window the shadow run scored, so
+offline and shadow numbers are directly comparable. A random split on this
+data flatters every model in it, and would have hidden the failure in §9
+entirely: with rows from the same card on both sides of the split, a
+whole-table card aggregate looks like a legitimate feature.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Features
+
+Seven features. The column that matters is **when the value becomes known** —
+it is the column this candidate failed on.
+
+| Feature | Source | Known at | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `amount_log` | authorization message | authorization | `log1p(amount_minor)`, integer minor units |
+| `hour_of_day` | authorization timestamp | authorization | |
+| `is_night` | authorization timestamp | authorization | 01:00–05:00 local |
+| `is_cross_border` | authorization message | authorization | ⚠ geography proxy — see §5 |
+| `mcc_risk_tier` | MCC + published network risk table | authorization | static map in `ml/features/base.py`, revised by hand |
+| `amount_zscore_within_mcc` | amount + MCC | authorization | ⚠ **frame-dependent**: standardised against a mean and standard deviation computed over the whole frame. Uses no outcome data, so it is not leakage, but the two moments must be fit on the training window and frozen before ship. Tracked below. |
+| `card_chargeback_rate` | `card_history` nightly batch | **as of the last batch refresh — up to 24h stale** | ⚠ **historical aggregate.** Point-in-time by construction: `ml/features/aggregates.py` counts only chargebacks *filed* strictly before the as-of instant, over that card's transactions that had *happened* strictly before it. Contributes nothing — see §4. |
+
+**Aggregate `as_of` cutoffs.** `card_chargeback_rate` is the only aggregate.
+Training builds it with `as_of = df["timestamp"]` — each row as of its own
+scoring instant. Serving builds it with a scalar `as_of` — the batch refresh
+time. Both bounds are strict, so a transaction can never count its own
+chargeback and tied timestamps cannot change the answer.
+
+**Batch staleness.** The `card_history` job refreshes nightly, so at serving
+time the feature is between 0 and 24 hours old. A card disputed this morning
+looks clean until tomorrow's run. That is acceptable given the feature is
+worth nothing; it would not be acceptable if it were load-bearing.
+
+**Proxy attributes.** `is_cross_border` (and the `country` field it derives
+from) is a geography proxy; amount is a ticket-size proxy that correlates with
+income. Both are sliced in §5.
+
+**Not in the feature set, deliberately:** `device_card_count` from notebook 01.
+It is a genuine signal — the synthetic generator gives shared "mule" devices a
++2.3 logit — but the notebook computes it as a whole-timeline
+`groupby("device_id").card_token.nunique()`, which has the same defect as the
+feature in §9 in a weaker form. It needs an as-of cut in
+`ml/features/aggregates.py` before it goes in, at which point Gate 1 will
+check it.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Performance
+
+Metric is **PR-AUC**. At a 3.5% base rate, ROC-AUC barely moves when precision
+in the alert queue collapses; both are reported, PR-AUC is the one the gate
+enforces.
+
+Point-in-time features, trained on the 42,000-row training window, evaluated
+on the 18,000-row scored window:
+
+| | PR-AUC | ROC-AUC |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| **fraud-v3-candidate (point-in-time)** | **0.1590** | **0.7788** |
+| fraud-v2 champion, same window | 0.1640 | 0.7782 |
+| fraud-v3-candidate **without** `card_chargeback_rate` | 0.1602 | 0.7802 |
+| *(withdrawn)* fraud-v3 offline, leaky feature | *0.3578* | *0.9059* |
+| *(reproduced)* fraud-v3 shadow, trained leaky / served point-in-time | 0.0706 | 0.6992 |
+
+**The candidate does not beat the champion.** On PR-AUC it is 0.005 behind.
+Adding `card_chargeback_rate` to the feature set *costs* 0.0012 PR-AUC and
+0.0014 ROC-AUC — it is noise with a positive-sounding name, and its
+correlation with the label on training rows, computed point-in-time, is
+**+0.0017** (the leaky version's was +0.285).
+
+At the operating point — top 1% of scores, threshold 0.2628, 189 alerts:
+
+| | value |
+|---|---:|
+| recall @ 1% review budget | 10.08% |
+| precision @ 1% review budget | 33.86% |
+
+By month, because a model that only works on average stops working in month
+three:
+
+| month | rows | fraud rate | PR-AUC | ROC-AUC | recall @ 1% |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 2026-07 | 9,074 | 3.60% | 0.1541 | 0.7683 | 8.87% |
+| 2026-08 | 8,926 | 3.45% | 0.1663 | 0.7900 | 11.36% |
+
+Two months is not a stability claim. It is the entire scored window available.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Fairness
+
+Tessera holds no protected-class attributes on an authorization, and this
+repository holds no cardholder data, so nothing below is a protected-class
+measurement. These are the proxies available on the authorization record. A
+statutory adverse-impact analysis needs attributes we deliberately do not
+hold and is run by Compliance separately.
+
+Every number is at the fixed 1% review budget, on the scored window.
+Regenerate with `uv run python -m ml.validation.fairness --report fairness.md`.
+
+**Alert-rate spread by dimension**
+
+| dimension | ratio | highest | lowest |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| amount_band | 173.5x | Q4 > $70 — 3.84% | Q1 ≤ $13 — 0.02% |
+| geography | 16.5x | GB — 7.77% | US — 0.47% |
+| cross_border | 16.5x | cross-border — 7.77% | domestic — 0.47% |
+| time_of_day | 13.0x | night 01–05 — 3.90% | day — 0.30% |
+| mcc_risk_tier | 5.7x | tier 5 — 1.94% | tier 1 — 0.34% |
+
+**Geography**
+
+| slice | n | fraud rate | alert rate | TPR | FPR | precision | PR-AUC |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| GB (cross-border) | 1,428 | 10.15% | 7.77% | 26.90% | 5.61% | 35.14% | 0.2860 |
+| US (domestic) | 16,572 | 2.96% | 0.47% | 5.10% | 0.33% | 32.05% | 0.1190 |
+
+**Ticket size**
+
+| slice | n | fraud rate | alert rate | TPR | precision | PR-AUC |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| Q1 ≤ $13 | 4,513 | 1.09% | 0.02% | **0.00%** | 0.00% | 0.0278 |
+| Q2 $13–30 | 4,450 | 1.93% | 0.07% | **0.00%** | 0.00% | 0.0438 |
+| Q3 $30–70 | 4,484 | 3.19% | 0.22% | 2.80% | 40.00% | 0.1211 |
+| Q4 > $70 | 4,553 | 7.84% | 3.84% | 16.81% | 34.29% | 0.2160 |
+
+**Time of day**: night 01–05 TPR 19.64% (n 3,739) against day TPR 2.54%
+(n 14,261).
+
+**Read it this way.** Cross-border traffic is alerted 16.5× as often as
+domestic, against a fraud rate only 3.4× higher: GB cardholders absorb more
+friction per unit of risk than the ratio of base rates justifies, and its FPR
+is 5.61% against 0.33% domestic. That is defensible on the numbers — precision
+holds at 35% — but it is a disparity, it is not small, and it should be a
+conscious decision rather than a discovered one.
+
+The clearer defect is the other end: **the bottom two ticket-size quartiles
+catch no fraud at all** at this budget. 49 and 86 frauds respectively, zero
+detected. Small-ticket fraud — including card-testing, which is small by
+definition — is not covered by this model at a 1% budget. Alert-rate parity is
+not the right fix; a second, cheaper rule for low-value card testing is.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Limitations and failure modes
+
+1. **The candidate is not better than the champion** (§4). There is no
+   performance argument for promoting it.
+2. **`card_chargeback_rate` contributes nothing.** Computed correctly it
+   carries no signal. It cannot be recovered by reweighting or recalibration —
+   the information simply is not there at scoring time. The recommendation is
+   to drop it from the v3 feature set; it is retained in the validated
+   pipeline only so the gates keep measuring it while the decision is open.
+3. **A failed batch looks like a clean card.** If the nightly `card_history`
+   job does not run, the feature serves 0.0, which is indistinguishable from
+   "this card has no history". The model reads a broken pipeline as
+   reassurance. Given the feature's weight this is currently harmless; it
+   becomes a real failure mode the moment any history feature matters. Alarm
+   on batch age (§7), not on the feature value.
+4. **Thin files are conflated with clean files.** A card with no prior
+   transactions scores 0.0, the same value as a card with a long clean
+   history. There is no "unknown" state.
+5. **Cold start shifts the feature between training and production.** The
+   point-in-time rate is non-zero on 3.44% of training rows against 14.19% of
+   scored rows, because cards accumulate history as the timeline runs
+   (PSI 0.16, §7). This is a real distribution difference, not an error — and
+   it is exactly the kind of gap that gets misread as drift when it is
+   structural.
+6. **`amount_zscore_within_mcc` is fit over the whole frame** (§3). Freeze the
+   moments on the training window before ship.
+7. **No device-graph feature** (§3), so the strongest remaining signal in the
+   data is unused.
+8. **Two months of evaluation.** Fraud is non-stationary; nothing here speaks
+   to behaviour beyond 54 days.
+9. **Synthetic data throughout.** These numbers validate the pipeline, not the
+   model's production performance.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Monitoring plan
+
+**Pre-merge** — `.github/workflows/model-validation.yml` runs four gates on
+every change under `ml/`. All four pass on this candidate:
+
+| Gate | Command | Current |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1. point-in-time | `python -m ml.validation.leakage --fail-on-leak` | PASS — 15 checks, no feature moves under label permutation, outcome censoring, or future-row deletion |
+| 2. performance | `python -m ml.validation.performance --min-pr-auc 0.15` | PASS — PR-AUC 0.1590 (floor 0.15, margin 6%) |
+| 3. drift | `python -m ml.validation.drift --max-psi 0.25` | PASS — worst PSI 0.1649, `card_chargeback_rate` |
+| 4. fairness | `python -m ml.validation.fairness --report fairness.md` | PASS — report-only; §5 |
+
+Gate 2's margin is thin by construction: the floor is the champion's PR-AUC
+and this candidate is level with the champion. A regression of 0.01 fails CI.
+
+**In production**
+
+| Signal | Cadence | Threshold | Action |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Score distribution PSI vs training | daily | > 0.10 review, > 0.25 page | risk-gateway on-call, `ops/runbooks/risk-gateway.md` |
+| Per-feature PSI | daily | as above | ml-fraud |
+| `card_chargeback_rate` non-zero share | daily | outside 10–20% | ml-fraud — a step change here means the batch definition moved, which is what TESS-2310 was |
+| `card_history` batch age at scoring | continuous | > 26h | page — the feature is silently zero past this point |
+| Alert rate vs the 1% budget | hourly | ±50% of budget | risk-gateway on-call |
+| Per-slice alert rate (§5 dimensions) | weekly | 25% change in any dimension's ratio | ml-fraud |
+| Precision in the review queue | weekly | < 25% | ml-fraud |
+| PR-AUC on matured labels | monthly, 90-day lag | < 0.15 | ml-fraud |
+
+Note the lag on the last row and why the rows above it exist. Disputes arrive
+20–89 days after the transaction, so accuracy metrics cannot be an alarm —
+by the time PR-AUC moves, two months of decisions have already been made.
+Drift and feature-population monitors are the actual alarm; label-based
+metrics are confirmation after the fact.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Decisions and explanations
+
+This model produces a fraud score, not a credit decision. A fraud decline is
+not an adverse action under ECOA/Reg B, and no adverse-action notice or
+reason-code mapping is required or provided. If this model is ever pointed at
+a credit decision, that mapping and its evidence must be built first, and this
+card does not cover that use (§1).
+
+Score consumption:
+
+| Band | Action |
+|---|---|
+| ≥ decline threshold | declined at `services/risk_gateway`, cardholder sees a generic decline |
+| top ~1% of scores, below decline | manual review queue |
+| otherwise | approved |
+
+Support and the cardholder see a decline category, not a score. One
+constraint on those categories: **do not surface a reason that names card
+dispute history.** The feature that would justify such a reason contributes
++0.0017 correlation — a reason code citing it would not be the real reason,
+and a reason code that is not the real reason is the problem regardless of
+which regulation applies.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Validation history
+
+**2026-09-16 — promotion blocked.** Offline the candidate scored AUC 0.906 /
+PR-AUC 0.358. In shadow over 2026-07-05 → 2026-08-27 it scored 0.699 / 0.071,
+*below* the 0.778 / 0.164 champion. Report:
+`ml/registry/shadow/fraud-v3-candidate.json`.
+
+**Root cause — target leakage plus train/serve skew in one feature.**
+`card_chargeback_rate` was defined in `ml/notebooks/01_fraud_exploration.ipynb`
+cell 8 as a whole-table aggregate:
+
+```python
+df.groupby("card_token").chargeback_filed_at.apply(lambda s: s.notna().mean())
+```
+
+No as-of cut. `chargeback_filed_at` is non-null on 1,777 rows and **every one
+of them is fraud** — it is the label under another name — and it is populated
+20–89 days after the transaction. So every training row was handed
+chargebacks filed after it, including the one filed against itself. Offline,
+train and test both got the leaky version and the leak scored itself: 0.906.
+Production serves the point-in-time version. Training saw the feature
+non-zero on 27.43% of rows; serving saw 14.19%. In training the feature
+selected a slice with 18.8× the fraud rate, so the model made it the dominant
+split; at serving the same condition selected a slice with 0.97× the fraud
+rate. The model's strongest split was firing at random, which is why the
+candidate landed *below* a champion that never had the feature.
+
+Full analysis: `ml/notebooks/03_v3_shadow_investigation.ipynb`. It reproduces
+all three numbers — offline 0.9059/0.3578, shadow 0.6992/0.0706, honest
+0.7775/0.1562 — to four decimals.
+
+**2026-09-20 — productionised (this card).** The point-in-time implementation
+lives in `ml/features/aggregates.py` with tests in `ml/features/tests/`; the
+four gates live in `ml/validation/`. The notebook's definition is kept as a
+negative fixture in `ml/validation/tests/negative_fixtures.py`, and Gate 1
+fails on it — that failure is itself a test, so the gate cannot rot into a
+no-op.
+
+**Recommendation to MRM.** Keep the promotion blocked. Drop
+`card_chargeback_rate` from the v3 feature set — point-in-time it is worth
+−0.0012 PR-AUC. Give `device_card_count` an as-of cut and re-run: the device
+signal is real and currently unused, and it is the only candidate change in
+front of us with a plausible path past the champion.
